**Reported by [#51](https://github.com/JohanSardar/argus/issues/51)'s worker while fixing the same gap in the Claude source. Reported, deliberately not fixed there — it belongs in its own ticket with its own grading.**

## The gap

`src/tailing/hermes.ts` never resolves or emits `repo`. `HermesParser` emits `cwd`, `parentSessionId`, `model` and `ts` — but not the worktree→parent-repo mapping.

This completes a pattern worth stating, because it explains why none of it was caught:

| source | `repo` resolution |
|---|---|
| Codex (`src/tailing/codex.ts`) | had it — `repositoryFor` |
| Claude (`src/tailing/claude-code.ts`) | **added by #51**, sharing the same helper |
| Hermes (`src/tailing/hermes.ts`) | **still missing** |

Every fixture in the tree uses a plain repo path, so no test ever needed the mapping. It became visible only when a real session running in a real git **worktree** was read — and Argus's own build runs every worker in `~/argus-worktrees/<slug>`, exactly the shape that needs it.

## Scope

1. Give the Hermes source the same worktree→parent-repo resolution. **Share `repositoryFor`** — #51 exported it from `src/tailing/codex.ts` for exactly this reason. A second implementation is how the two sources drifted apart in the first place; do not write one.
2. **Red test first, pinning a real worktree shape** — `git init` a parent, commit, `git worktree add` a linked worktree, use it as the session's `cwd`. Not a hand-written fixture path. Fixtural blindness is the root cause here and a fixture-shaped test would reproduce it while appearing to fix it.
3. **Grade it precisely.** Name the §13 scenario that owns repository attribution (#51 identified **scenario 3, Rogue session**, registered by [#22](https://github.com/JohanSardar/argus/issues/22)) and say whether its verdict changes — and if it does not, say that plainly. Capability moving is not the same as a numbered scenario moving; see [#49](https://github.com/JohanSardar/argus/issues/49).
4. If Hermes's `cwd` can be absent or shaped differently from Claude's and Codex's, say so — [#49](https://github.com/JohanSardar/argus/issues/49) found exactly that kind of difference in real Claude transcripts (records carrying a `sessionId` and no `cwd` at all), and the equivalent for Hermes has never been checked against real output.

## Hard rules

**Never write to the real `~/.hermes`** — scratch `HERMES_HOME` only for anything created; the real tree is read-only and only if it exists. Never touch `~/.hermes/.env` or `auth.json` beyond a read a scratch run legitimately needs, and never print their contents. **Do not create `~/.hermes/profiles/` on the real machine and do not run `argus up` against a real repo** — that is a deployment decision and it is Johan's. Never read, copy or reuse `~/.claude/.credentials.json`; never run `claude setup-token`, `/login`, `logout` or `claude auth`. SPEC §1 item 7: Argus must never require Anthropic API access — any live drive uses subscription mode, never a token. Never kill a process this ticket did not spawn.

[#47](https://github.com/JohanSardar/argus/issues/47) built a self-provisioning live Hermes verification behind a three-status gate — **reuse it** rather than inventing a second path, and keep its skips loud and reasoned.

## Method and gates

TDD, red first. Keep the change minimal — a gap-fill, not a redesign of the tailing layer. `bun install` first. Gates: `bun x tsc --noEmit` + `bun test` green with gates unset, zero runtime dependencies.
